      "text": "To determine how many different positive four-digit integers can be formed using the digits 2, 2, 9, and 9, we need to consider the permutations of these digits where repetition is allowed.\n\nFirst, calculate the total number of permutations of the digits 2, 2, 9, and 9. This is given by the formula for permutations of a multiset:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n!}{n_1! \\cdot n_2! \\cdots n_k!}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( n \\) is the total number of items to arrange, and \\( n_1, n_2, \\ldots, n_k \\) are the frequencies of the distinct items. Here, \\( n = 4 \\) (the total number of digits), and the digits 2 and 9 each appear twice. Thus, the formula becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{4!}{2! \\cdot 2!}\n\\]\n\nCalculate the factorials:\n\n\\[\n4! = 4 \\times 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 24\n\\]\n\\[\n2! = 2 \\times 1 = 2\n\\]\n\nSubstitute these values back into the formula:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{24}{2 \\cdot 2} = \\frac{24}{4} = 6\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the number of different positive four-digit integers that can be formed using the digits 2, 2, 9, and 9 is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).",
